      "content": "The Electoral College plays a crucial role in the uniquely American process of presidential elections. Upon the presidential election's results, the candidate requiring the majority of the votes (at least 270 electoral votes) is declared the winner. This system disproportionately represents and gives varying weight to votes in different states, with larger states having more electoral votes than smaller ones. This creates electoral disparities where candidates may win the national popular vote without securing the presidency, influencing the country's democratic system in several ways: limiting national representation, favoring swing states, and incentivizing campaign strategies focused on regional victories.",
